# Close out the current issue

Run this when implementation is done and you are ready to land the work (commit, push, PR). Post-merge branch hygiene now lives in a separate command, `/iflow-cleanup`, which you run **after** the PR is merged.

{% include "skills/_model_directive.md.j2" %}

{% include "skills/_resolve_project_root.md.j2" %}

## Input

Optional text after the command (same line). Examples:

- **No extra text** — close the issue without bumping the package version.
- **`bump`** (no level) — bump using the **pre-release-aware default**: stay on the current release channel (alpha→alpha, beta→beta, rc→rc, dev→dev) or **patch** when the current version is already stable. See `iflow-version-bump`.
- **A named level** — `patch`, `minor`, `major`, `stable`, `alpha`, `beta`, `rc`, `post`, or `dev` (e.g. `bump minor`, `beta`) → use exactly that `uv version --bump <level>`. `dev` must be paired with another component (e.g. `bump patch dev`).
- **Free text** — if it clearly asks to release or bump the version, infer the level from wording (e.g. "bugfix release" → `patch`, "promote to beta" → `beta`); if unclear, ask once. Never auto-pick `major`.
- **`nohistory`** or **`skip history`** — skip the `{{ history_file }}` update step (step 3) for this run.
- **`log "..."`** or **`note "..."`** — use the quoted text verbatim as the `{{ history_file }}` bullet summary (otherwise the GitHub issue title is used).
- **`stay`**, **`stay on branch`**, **`don't switch`**, or **`dont switch to main`** — after the PR step, stay on the issue branch instead of switching back to the default branch.
- **`yolo`** — hands-off close (used by `/iflow-yolo`): decide the `{{ history_file }}` update yourself without a confirm prompt (step 3), **merge the PR** after opening/updating it (step 7a), then switch back to the default branch and pull (step 8). See the per-step notes below.
- **`draft`** — when creating a PR in step 7, use `gh pr create --draft`. If an open PR already exists, leave it draft (do not mark ready). **`draft` skips yolo merge** entirely (step 7a).

Other optional notes still apply: branch name, PR title, skip issue doc update, commit all changes, etc.

## Typical steps

1. **Sanity check**
   - Run tests and any checks you rely on (e.g. `uv run pytest`).
{% if ruff_autofix %}   - **Ruff (when present).** If the project uses ruff (`[tool.ruff]` in `pyproject.toml`, ruff in dev dependencies, or `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ designs_folder }}/python-quality-tools.md` exists), run auto-fix lint through the documented Python runner before committing — e.g. `uv run ruff check --fix …` then `uv run ruff format …` (match paths to what the project documents).
{% endif %}   - Skim the diff so the commit matches what you intend to ship.
   - Confirm that any design decisions or good-practices that emerged from this issue are captured under `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ designs_folder }}/` before committing. If something is missing, add it now (short markdown: context, decision, alternatives, link back to the issue).
{% if suggest_graphify %}   - **Graph freshness (optional).** If this change touched the project's structure (new modules, big refactor, removed files) and `graphify-out/` exists, suggest the user run `/iflow-graphify` (default: `graphify update`, AST-only, no LLM key needed) before pushing so teammates pull a current `GRAPH_REPORT.md`. Do not run `/iflow-graphify` automatically — it is opt-in. Skip this bullet entirely if `graphify-out/` is not present.
{% endif %}
2. **Optional version bump** (only if the user asked for it in the command input{% if confirm_version_bump %}; without `yolo`, if they did not ask, ask once whether to bump{% endif %})
   - Read `{{ agent_dir }}/skills/iflow-version-bump/SKILL.md` and follow it — it resolves the project's **release strategy** first (the "Release & version bump" section of `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ designs_folder }}/this-project.md`, else `pyproject.toml` detection, else the uv default), and defines every supported level and the pre-release-aware default.
   - **Static version:** from the project root, run `uv version --bump <level>` for the resolved level (`bump` with no level → the default rule based on the current version; or the named level from the input). Do this **before** updating issue markdown and **before** commit / push / PR so the new version is in the tree that gets merged.
   - **Git-tag derived:** edit nothing — compute and report the **planned tag** (next version from the latest tag, e.g. `v1.0.4a3`), record it in the status file, and defer creating the tag until after the merge (step 8 with `yolo`, else `/iflow-cleanup`). Never tag the issue branch.
   - If neither strategy applies (no `pyproject.toml` version at all), skip and say so; continue with the remaining steps.

3. **Update `{{ history_file }}`** (opt-out via `nohistory`)
   - Read `{{ agent_dir }}/skills/iflow-history-update/SKILL.md` and follow it.
   - Default summary for the new bullet is the GitHub issue title; override with `log "..."` / `note "..."` from the command input.
   - If step 2 did **not** bump (or plan) a version, append a bullet to the existing `## [Unreleased]` section: `- <summary>. (#<N>)`.
   - If step 2 **did** bump or plan a version, promote `## [Unreleased]` to `## [<new_version>] - <YYYY-MM-DD>` (for tag-derived projects use the planned tag's version), prepend a fresh empty `## [Unreleased]` above it, and put the new bullet inside the just-closed release section.
   - If `{{ history_file }}` does not exist at the project root, skip this step with a short note and continue. Never create it here.
{% if confirm_changelog_update %}   - Show the proposed diff and confirm once before writing. If declined, **stop** — offer write / revise / `nohistory` / abort; do not continue to commit or PR without an explicit choice.
{% else %}   - Write without a confirm prompt (`confirm_changelog_update` is false) so the bullet lands in the PR commit. Still report what was written.
{% endif %}   - **`yolo` input:** do not ask — decide yourself and write the bullet (issue title, or the `log "..."` text) directly. `nohistory` still skips the step entirely.
   - Write this step **even when a draft PR already exists** from `/iflow-build` early PR — the bullet must land in the close commit that updates that PR.
   - **Never** propose updating `{{ history_file }}` after close has finished or after merge.

4. **Issue tracking in the repo** (see project rules under `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ current_issues_folder }}`)
   - Update the status file for this issue: clear checklist, remaining work, and use `- [x] Done` only when fully resolved.
   - If the issue is fully resolved, move its markdown files from `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ current_issues_folder }}` to `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ solved_folder }}`. If partially resolved, move to `{{ issueflows_dir }}/{{ partly_solved_folder }}`.

5. **Commit and fix merge conflicts**
   - Before staging, run `git status` to list all modified/untracked files. If any changes are **not relevant** to this issue, tell the user which ones and ask whether to include them in this commit or leave them for later. Do not silently drop or include unrelated changes.
   - Unless told to commit all, stage the right files (avoid unrelated changes). Include `pyproject.toml` (and `uv.lock` if it changed) when a version bump ran. Include `{{ history_file }}` when step 3 updated it.
   - Write a commit message that states what changed and why in normal sentences.
   - Sync with the default branch before pushing: run `git fetch --prune` then `git pull --ff-only` from the default branch (e.g. `main`) merged into the issue branch (or rebase, per project preference). Use `--ff-only` so unrelated work never gets merged in silently; if it refuses, stop and ask how to reconcile. Check for and fix merge conflicts.

6. **Push**
   - Push your branch to `origin` (or the remote you use).

7. **Pull request**
   - Always pass `--repo <owner/repo>`.
   - **List before create.** `gh pr list --repo <owner/repo> --head <branch> --state open --json number,url,title,isDraft`. If an open PR already exists for this head (including a draft from `/iflow-build` early PR), **update** it instead of opening a second one; otherwise `gh pr create` — add `--draft` when the user passed the `draft` token.
   - Describe the change, how to test it, and link the GitHub issue (prefer `Closes #123` when shipping; `Refs #123` is fine for WIP).
   - **Ready from draft (when not `draft`).** If the open PR is still a draft and the user did **not** pass `draft`, mark it ready for review (`gh pr ready <number> --repo <owner/repo>`) before the checks snapshot / yolo merge.
   - **Checks snapshot.** After the PR exists, run `gh pr checks <number> --repo <owner/repo>` and report pass / fail / pending. "CI is green" means exit 0 (or JSON buckets all `pass` / `skipping`). Without `yolo`, prefer the one-shot list; offer `gh pr checks … --watch --fail-fast` only when the user wants to wait, still honouring the **{{ checks_watch_minutes }}-minute** wall-clock cap (agent-enforced).{% if "gh_ci" in included_skills %} Full CI/`gh` cheatsheet (including `gh run list` / `gh run watch` fallback when PR checks are empty): `{{ agent_dir }}/skills/gh-ci/SKILL.md`.{% endif %} If `gh pr checks` returns empty or cannot resolve checks, fall back to `gh run list --repo <owner/repo>` then `gh run watch <run-id> --repo <owner/repo>` under the same budget.

7a. **Merge the PR (`yolo` input only)**
   - Never pass `--delete-branch` — branch deletion stays in `/iflow-cleanup`. Without `yolo`, skip — merging stays a user decision (step 9).
   - If the user passed `draft`, **skip merge entirely** and say so.
   - Try `gh pr merge <number> --{{ pr_merge_method }}` immediately (baked from `[issueflow].pr_merge_method`).
   - If GitHub refuses for pending/required checks: `gh pr checks <number> --repo <owner/repo> --watch --fail-fast` under a hard wall-clock budget of **{{ checks_watch_minutes }} minutes** (from `[issueflow].checks_watch_minutes` / `ISSUEFLOW_CHECKS_WATCH_MINUTES`, default 15; agent stops the watch when the cap hits — `gh` has no max-duration flag).
   - Watch succeeds within the cap → retry `gh pr merge <number> --{{ pr_merge_method }}`.
   - Watch fails (red / `--fail-fast`) → stop hands-off, leave the PR open, report failing check links.
   - Cap elapses while still pending, or checks never register / watch unavailable → last resort `gh pr merge <number> --{{ pr_merge_method }} --auto` and report the merge as queued. If even `--auto` fails, stop hands-off, report the error, leave the PR open.

8. **Switch back when safe**
   - If the input included `stay`, `stay on branch`, `don't switch`, or `dont switch to main`, stay on the issue branch and report that the user opted out.
{% if not auto_switchback %}   - This project has `auto_switchback = false`, so **stay on the issue branch by default** unless the user explicitly asks to switch. Planned release tags for tag-derived + `yolo` wait for `/iflow-cleanup`.
{% else %}   - **CLI fast path (preferred).** If the `issue-flow` CLI is on `PATH`, run `issue-flow agent switchback --json` after the PR is open or updated. It performs this whole step deterministically: refuses while the working tree is dirty (listing the paths), else switches to the detected default branch and runs `git pull --ff-only`. On exit 1, report its `notes` to the user and stop — do not force anything.
   - **Manual fallback.** Detect the default branch: prefer `gh repo view --json defaultBranchRef -q .defaultBranchRef.name`, else `git symbolic-ref --quiet --short refs/remotes/origin/HEAD | sed 's|^origin/||'`, else `main`. Run `git status --porcelain`; if it is clean, switch back with `git switch <default>` and then run `git pull --ff-only`. A clean tree here means the branch work has been committed and pushed to the PR branch. If the working tree is dirty, stay on the current branch, list the uncommitted paths, and explain that switching is unsafe until those changes are committed, stashed, or discarded by the user.
   - **`yolo` input:** this step is mandatory (unless `stay` was also passed) and runs **after** the merge from step 7a, so the pull brings the just-merged commit into the local default branch. If step 7a only queued an auto-merge, still switch and pull, and note that the merge commit will arrive once checks pass.
   - **Planned release tag (`yolo` + tag-derived strategy only):** if step 2 planned a tag, create it now — after the pull, standing on the merge commit — with `git tag <planned>` then `git push origin <planned>` (covered by the yolo consolidated confirm). If the merge was only queued via `--auto`, leave the tag to `/iflow-cleanup` and say so.
{% endif %}   - This step never deletes the issue branch. Branch deletion remains in `/iflow-cleanup`.

9. **After review**
   - With the `yolo` input the PR was already merged in step 7a —{% if remind_cleanup %} skip to the `/iflow-cleanup` reminder below.{% else %} skip further merge work.{% endif %}
   - Address feedback, push updates, and merge when approved and `gh pr checks <number> --repo <owner/repo>` is green (exit 0).
   - If step 8 switched back to the default branch, switch to the PR branch again before making review fixes.
{% if remind_cleanup %}   - Once the PR is merged, run **`/iflow-cleanup`** to switch back to the default branch, `git pull --ff-only`, `git fetch --prune`, and delete local branches whose commits are already in the default branch (single consolidated confirm — which, for tag-derived projects, also offers to create the release tag planned in step 2). `/iflow-close` no longer does post-merge cleanup itself.
{% endif %}
## Constraints

- **Changelog timing:** unless `nohistory`, write `{{ history_file }}` in step 3 and stage it in the close commit that feeds (or updates) the PR — including when a draft was opened earlier via `/iflow-build` early PR. Never offer a HISTORY/CHANGELOG update after close has finished or after merge.
{% if confirm_changelog_update %}- If the changelog confirm is declined, **stop** until the user chooses write / revise / `nohistory` / abort — do not silent-skip and continue.
{% endif %}- Never delete branches from `/iflow-close`. Branch deletion belongs to `/iflow-cleanup`.

## Output

Summarize what was committed, pushed, the PR URL (or next step if blocked), and whether the working copy switched back to the default branch or stayed on the issue branch. With the `yolo` input, also report the merge result (merged, or queued via `--auto`).{% if remind_cleanup %} Remind the user to run `/iflow-cleanup` after the PR merges (or right away when yolo already merged it).{% endif %}
